okay so i i'm going to  uh talk about a new uh research effort  uh that i call hybridizing machine  learning just to talk about some of the  the things that are relevant for us in  the  insurance industry but i thought it'd be  useful just because a lot of people are  not familiar with what a re-insurer is  or  what an uh how that relates to the  insurance industry just to talk briefly  about that first i'm actually  with the swiss re institute uh i also  head up the the research and engagement  uh component of the swiss re institute  we have  offices around the world uh usually i'm  i'm  spending some of my time in san  francisco and new york and  and some of our offices in asia but uh  obviously now i'm  grounded in zurich which is not  necessarily a bad place to be  uh but uh we we are the the r d  component  of the reinsurer and for those of you  not familiar  with how the the industry is set up our  clients are actually  other insurance companies so direct  insurers take specific types of risk  and uh this is a quote that i like going  all the way back to the 20th century  that you know essentially without uh  insurance and effectively reinsurers  it'd be very hard  to take very large risks such as  building skyscrapers but also  ensuring hurricanes ensuring earthquakes  uh all the big risks and we essentially  make money  by allocating capital across uh  different types of risk categories and  then  essentially collecting the premiums and  investing those  in our our asset portfolio so that we  have money to pay out  when when the the downside events occur  so there's lots of  prediction pattern recognition that's  generally a component of insurance  historically it's been driven by the  actuarial  science but in recent years  there's been a shift in the industry  where we think  uh i mean we still we still try to  understand the risks and cover them  but that's becoming much more now  predict and prevent  and as a consequence that the we're  looking into much more than just  insurance and and some of these new  modeling techniques we think could be  quite effective in facilitating new  businesses  that we like to call resilience as a  service now to get there  uh there's a lot of hurdles in the real  world that you don't face  in in academia and actually i'm i'm also  an academic so i kind of understand both  sides of this this picture  but one of the big things is a lot of  the data that's quite useful  for these calculations are subject to  very  strict uh privacy regulations in europe  we have something called gdpr  that restricts how we can use data and  so this area of confidential computing i  just mentioned this because  it's becoming a bigger and bigger issue  and  there's still a number of paths of  research which  uh we're involved in some i don't have  time to go in through  go through the details but uh in  in broad terms you're either trying to  create trusted execution environments  in hardware such as intel's sgx  protocols  uh on their chips or you're trying to  manipulate  uh fully homomorphically encrypted data  there's a few other things you can  create trusted execution environments  and clouds  et cetera but this is a real issue and  so this is actually one dimension of  hybridization  whatever we do on the machine  intelligence it often has to be  hybridized with some kind of  confidential computing now there's one  path that we're  quite excited about we have a few  projects underway  that that facilitates uh this type of  confidential computing using  edge compute services or federated  learning  uh and if i had more time i could go  into the details  with this is still very much in the  early stages and  and we're seeing challenges deploying it  at enterprise scale which is a common  uh theme for uh reinsurance but the edge  computing the federated learning for  those of you interested in this area  this this is  this is quite important at least we  think to the future of how we can  deploy on these large enterprise  networks that we're part of  now uh one of my favorite authors is an  evolutionary biologist  by the game by the the name of eo wilson  and he was one time  quoted as saying you know humans have  paleolithic emotions medieval  institutions and god-like technologies  now if i take the analog in my industry  we have poorly curated data  and actually we have a lot of it but  it's it's uh it's not curated very well  we've got fragmented technology  architectures and then we've got a whole  bunch of god-like algorithms  and one of the challenges that we have  is we see a lot of innovative stuff out  of academia  out of our startup partners these  godlike algorithms but not enough people  thinking about how do you deal  with incomplete data how do you deal  with poorly curated  data and fragmented architectures this  is a real issue  and so i i make a big distinction  between  the ability to run some kind of a  an algorithm whether it be reinforcement  learning or deep learning  where you've got lots of data versus the  world that i live in  where i actually have lots of data but  it's just not well managed  and so that's that's why we're we find  these hybridized machine learning  approaches to be quite  interesting and and we have a a new  project area which we  loosely call the risk intelligence  factory where we're trying to take  a lot of these different networks that  are developing  many of them are implementing  distributed ledgers or blockchains  uh we have lots of internal data trying  to run that through confidential  computing  and then converting that into some kind  of resilience as a service  now this is an example of uh  a candidate architecture that's actually  something we're developing  uh together with a partner and this is  where you essentially  you start with some kind of a  physics-based model  such as say a hydrodynamic model for  urban resilience  and then you layer on top of that in a  in  a hybrid hybridized way different kinds  of machine learning  and this is something we're still  exploring could be reinforcement  learning  could be other kinds of approaches and  then  uh migrating that over to a simulation  setup that would allow us to look at  things like  what happens to an urban area when a  large flood  comes through or what happens when  there's a large earthquake  now if you look at the modeling in the  space you either have  very primitive actuarial models that are  that are based on parametrizations that  are mostly out of date particularly in  the context of climate change although  they're trying to update those  and then you have some people attempting  to run  i would say loosely supervised or almost  unsupervised approaches  but that often doesn't work real well  and then the straight physics-based  models  tend to be not granular enough to look  at uh  certain types of risks so this is an  example where this hybridization  can potentially really pay dividends at  least in in the risk assessment that  we're looking at  and then if you think if you move to the  other side of our balance sheet so that  that's  thinking more in terms of the liability  side if you move to our asset side of  our balance sheet often what we're  trying to do is predict certain types of  uh financial market variables such as  inflation such as interest rates  uh et cetera and hybrid is eight  sorry two minutes but  the hybridization uh here is really  about taking for example  subject matter expertise and  incorporating it  uh with some kind of behavioral cloning  or bayesian  updating and then potentially sticking  that inside of a reinforcement learning  harness  so that would be another idea that we  have for hybridization  these are all discussions that we're  having we're looking for people  interested in these types of projects so  this is why  you know i raised these points so just  in the end  at least from our perspective it's  important to realize that enterprise  scale is quite important  i've seen many uh promising algorithms  fail when we try to deploy them  end-to-end and enterprise uh contacts  because the latency  is too is too long and the costs are too  high either in terms of compute  or time uh and often we're dealing  with with just very fragmented data  value chains so that has to be taken  into account  and the confidential computing is is  always underlying  everything so these hybrid models we  believe could be  uh quite useful if we can figure out how  to make them work  so i will finish there and i don't know  if i have time for questions but  thank you very much for the opportunity  to present fantastic  thanks jeff

